<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Upload into a PASSWORD-PROTECTED PUBLIC SHARE on Nextcloud, through the four requests its web client
  makes. Verified end to end against stock Nextcloud 35, and request by request against a deployment
  reached through a FILTERING REVERSE PROXY - see the note at the end of this comment.

  THREE THINGS TO REPLACE, and nothing else:

    SHARE-HOST        https://foo.bar.com          the scheme and host, no trailing slash. Appears
                                                   twice: server= and the Origin header.
    SHARETOKEN        what follows /s/ in the URL. Appears FOUR times, and must be written
                      literally: a <rest> step's 'path' is NOT environment-expanded (only
                      <upload>'s own server / uri / proxy / proxy_bypass are), so a %VAR% there
                      would reach the wire unexpanded.
    SHARE-PASSWORD    the share password, in clear, as every embedded credential is.

  WHY FOUR REQUESTS, not the three a first reading of the dialog suggests:

    1. GET the authenticate page. It sets the session cookies and serves the CSRF token as
       data-requesttoken. Aimed straight at /authenticate/showshare rather than at /s/SHARETOKEN,
       which answers a redirect towards it that would otherwise have to be followed for no gain.
    2. POST the password. Nextcloud refuses this with 412 unless the token is supplied; a
       requesttoken header and a form field of the same name both work, and the header is used
       here. sharingToken and sharingType are what the web client sends: stock Nextcloud ignores
       them. Answers 303 towards the share.
    3. GET the share page, now authenticated. THIS STEP IS NOT OPTIONAL: the login regenerates the
       session id, so the token from step 1 is stale by here and the WebDAV write needs the one
       this page serves. The two differ on the wire.
    4. PUT the file under the public DAV root. Behind the filtering proxy this needs the cookie AND all
       headers below: cookie alone, and cookie plus requesttoken, were both answered 401. Stock
       Nextcloud needs only the cookie, so the headers cost nothing where they are not required.

  Cookies need no configuration: every step runs on one WinHTTP session handle whose cookie jar
  replays Set-Cookie across the four requests, __Host- and SameSite ones included.

  session="once" marks the first three steps as session establishment rather than per-file work, so
  the login runs once for the whole collection instead of replaying the password for every file.

  IF THE LOGIN IS REFUSED: a POST answered 200 instead of 302/303 was not processed at all - the
  usual cause is an instance that does NOT serve pretty URLs, where every /s/... path needs an
  /index.php prefix (the PUT path never does). Its own Location header says which form it uses.
  tests/RestAgent/Check-NextcloudShare.ps1 diagnoses that case, and tells it apart from a password
  the server actually refused.

  Certificate pinning is not set here. Add server_cert_pin="<base64 of the server certificate>" to
  the <upload> element to pin one; do not use insecure="true" against a real share, because the
  form body below carries the share password.

  DO NOT ADD offset= TO THE PUT BELOW. This is the one edit that looks like a resume and silently
  destroys data. Measured against Nextcloud 35, on a 5000 byte file already on the server:

    PUT of just the tail, no Content-Range     204, and the file becomes 4 bytes long
    PUT of just the tail WITH Content-Range    400 BadRequest, the file is left untouched
    PATCH + X-Update-Range (sabre partial)     501, "no plugin willing to handle this PATCH method",
                                               on the account endpoint too - Nextcloud never loads it

  So the naive shape is the dangerous one: offset= with no range header reports success and truncates
  the remote file to the size of the tail. Adding Content-Range only converts that into a loud 400; it
  never produces a resume, because Nextcloud has no partial write on this endpoint at all.

  A HEAD step CAN read the remote size here with a plain header capture and no WebDAV parsing:

      <rest http_method="HEAD" path="/public.php/dav/files/SHARETOKEN/{upload.filename}"
            accept_status="404">
          <header>X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest</header>
          <response header="Content-Length" name="have" default="0"/>
      </rest>

  200 with Content-Length for a file that is there, 404 for one that is not. That is a VERIFICATION
  value only - there is nothing in this dialog it can safely drive. Real resumption needs the chunk
  protocol: rest-nextcloud-share-resume.xml.

  BEHIND A FILTERING REVERSE PROXY. This dialog was developed against a deployment first believed to
  be a Nextcloud fork and later identified as ordinary Nextcloud behind a filtering reverse proxy.
  That explains every way it differed from the bench, and none of the differences are Nextcloud's:

    - Basic auth failed with the "no credential arrived" 401 (no WWW-Authenticate, empty
      <s:message/>): the proxy was not forwarding the Authorization header.
    - the PUT needed X-Requested-With where stock does not, which is the AJAX gate public DAV applies
      to any non-GET when outgoing server-to-server sharing is off.
    - chunked upload was unreachable: /public.php/dav/uploads/ refuses a session cookie, and Basic was
      unavailable for the reason above.

  This dialog is the one that survives such filtering, because it uses only what a browser uses: GET,
  POST and a single PUT under /public.php/dav/files/. Chunking and resuming need /uploads/ plus MKCOL,
  MOVE and PROPFIND, and whether those are reachable is the proxy's policy rather than Nextcloud's.
  Check-NextcloudProxy.ps1 measures exactly which of them a given deployment admits.
-->
<dfir-orc>
    <upload method="REST" server="https://SHARE-HOST" operation="copy"
            https_security="tls1.3, tls1.2">

        <rest http_method="GET" path="/s/SHARETOKEN/authenticate/showshare" session="once">
            <response attribute="data-requesttoken" name="rt1"/>
        </rest>

        <rest http_method="POST" path="/s/SHARETOKEN/authenticate/showshare"
              session="once" accept_status="302,303">
            <header>requesttoken: {rt1}</header>
            <form field="password"     value="SHARE-PASSWORD"/>
            <form field="sharingToken" value="SHARETOKEN"/>
            <form field="sharingType"  value="3"/>
        </rest>

        <rest http_method="GET" path="/s/SHARETOKEN" session="once">
            <response attribute="data-requesttoken" name="rt2"/>
        </rest>

        <rest http_method="PUT"
              path="/public.php/dav/files/SHARETOKEN/{upload.filename}"
              body="{upload.local_path}">
            <header>requesttoken: {rt2}</header>
            <header>X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest</header>
            <header>Origin: https://SHARE-HOST</header>
        </rest>

    </upload>
</dfir-orc>
